<speak>
<p>Our guiding case is u equal to three, so the order is thirty six. Assume, for contradiction, that a circulant Hadamard matrix of this order exists. Its thirty six sign coefficients are hypothetical. Their polynomial times its star equals thirty six.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Since four and nine are coprime, the Chinese remainder theorem identifies the thirty six cycle with a four cycle times a nine cycle. Replace X by Z times P. Coefficient j moves to the cell with row j modulo four and column j modulo nine. This reorganizes the same coefficients. These grid rows are not rows of the original matrix.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Collect each row of the grid as a polynomial in P. Call them H zero through H three. Each has nine sign coefficients. The original polynomial becomes H zero plus Z H one plus Z squared H two plus Z cubed H three.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>The fourth roots of unity respect Z to the fourth equals one. At Z equal to one, all four parts add. At negative one, their signs alternate. At i, the real part is H zero minus H two, and the imaginary part is H one minus H three. The value at negative i conjugates the coefficients, giving no independent fourth polynomial.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Divide each evaluation by two, and call the results c, d, and g. Dividing both a polynomial and its star by two divides their product by four. Thus thirty six becomes nine. Each of c, d, and g times its star equals nine. For Gaussian coefficients, star conjugates coefficients and reverses powers of P.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Why are there no fractions? Fix one power of P. For c and d, its numerator is a sum of four signs, possibly with alternating signs. That sum is even. For the real and imaginary parts of g, each numerator is a difference of two signs, also even. Therefore c and d have integer coefficients, and g has Gaussian integer coefficients.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Now substitute any ninth root of unity for P. Star becomes complex conjugation, so the norm identity says the squared absolute value of each output is nine. All three values lie on the circle of radius three. These are output values; the permitted inputs satisfy P to the ninth equals one. Their pictured positions are schematic.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For general odd u, the same construction gives norm u squared and output radius u. Return to u equal to three. We will compare the inputs one and omega, a cube root of unity. Both are ninth roots. The norm fixes distances; next we want to compare the changes in phase.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Here is a preview of Node six. Further information from the original signs will force one common rotation to carry all three outputs at one to their outputs at omega. The illustrated hundred and twenty degree rotation uses schematic starting points, not values from an actual order thirty six Hadamard matrix. This common rotation is a later conclusion, not a consequence of the norm alone.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
